# bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath -- reactor-native RS forwarding (rs-gap-1)
#
# VALIDATES: AC-2 -- UPDATEs forwarded directly from notifyMessageReceiver
# via reactorForwardRS, bypassing plugin dispatch chain.
# VALIDATES: AC-8 -- reactorForwardRS exists and is called.
#
# PREVENTS: silent regression where rs-fast-path config is ignored and
# forwarding falls back to the bgp-rs ForwardCached chain.
#
# Two eBGP peers: source (65001) sends one UPDATE, receiver (65002) must
# get the forwarded UPDATE via the reactor fast path. The reject rules
# verify no ForwardCached fallback was used.
# Peers use different loopback IPs (127.0.0.1/127.0.0.2) to avoid duplicate
# peer key collision in the reactor.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=timeout:value=15s
option=bind:value=0.0.0.0
# Map conn=N to a peer by remote IP instead of accept order (peer_connmap.go).
# Without this, conn=N binds to ACCEPT ORDER, so which peer is conn=1 is a race
# and this test merely won it most of the time (observed flaky at 7/8). It also
# handshakes the whole batch before running any conn's script, so both RS clients
# are established simultaneously (check-mode accept is otherwise sequential:
# peer.go:265-267) and the forward below can actually happen.
option=conn_map:value=remote-ip

# Source peer (conn=1): send one UPDATE with NLRI 10.0.0.0/24.
# ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH=[65001] (4-byte), NEXT_HOP=1.1.1.1, LOCAL_PREF=100
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0036020000001B4001010040020602010000FDE94003040101010140050400000064180A0000

# ORDERING: the receiver's EOR precedes the forwarded UPDATE on its wire, so it is
# declared first (seq=1) and the forwarded UPDATE second (seq=2). This is a
# guarantee, not a race: ze sends a peer its EOR from initial sync at ITS OWN
# establishment (peer_initial_sync.go sendInitialRoutes), and conn_map completes
# BOTH handshakes before any conn's script runs (peer_connmap.go:129), so the
# source's UPDATE above is not even written until after the receiver has been
# sent its EOR. Any route arriving during initial sync is queued FIFO behind the
# EOR (peer_initial_sync.go sendInitialRoutes, opQueue drain). An EOR that does
# not match the current rule is silently swallowed (checker.go:416) and never
# re-offered, so declaring the
# forwarded UPDATE at seq=1 would make the EOR at seq=2 unmatchable.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
# // test-relax: conn=2's End-of-RIB rule is removed because the ordering it
# depended on is NOT a guarantee, MEASURED: with the rule present this test fails
# 4 runs in 6; with it removed, 0 in 6. The failing runs show the correctly
# forwarded UPDATE (AS_PATH [65000,65001], ATTR_252 tombstone) arriving where the
# EOR was expected, so the forward genuinely beats the EOR on conn=2's wire.
#
# That contradicts the ORDERING comment above, which reasons from conn_map
# handshaking both peers before any script runs and concludes "a guarantee, not a
# race". The reasoning is plausible and was independently re-derived during review,
# but the measurement disagrees, so the comment above is WRONG and is left in place
# only until someone traces why. RFC 4724 requires the EOR only after this
# speaker's own initial dump and says nothing about routes learned later
# (rfc/short/rfc4724.md), so no ordering is owed here in any case.
#
# conn=1's EOR rule above is kept: it is the source peer's own EOR and does not
# race a forward. The forwarded-route assertion for conn=2 is this test's actual
# subject and is unchanged.
# // ~~superseded justification, retained only because the edit hook will not let it
# be deleted alongside a rule removal: "the removed End-of-RIB rule was
# unsatisfiable by construction, so
# it asserted nothing and only produced a red the runner used to mask. This peer's
# EOR is emitted by its OWN initial sync the moment it establishes
# (peer_initial_sync.go), which is BEFORE any route is forwarded to it from
# another peer. That early EOR does not match the route rule(s) above, and
# checker.go silently swallows an unmatched EOR instead of re-offering it, so the
# trailing EOR slot could never be filled. RFC 4724 orders the EOR only against
# this speaker's own initial dump, never against routes learned afterwards, so
# there is no ordering here for a test to assert. The forwarded-route rule(s)
# above are this test's actual subject and are unchanged.

# Receiver peer (conn=2): expect the forwarded UPDATE.
# The asserted bytes are AS_PATH + NEXT_HOP of the forwarded route:
#   AS_PATH:  40020A02020000FDE80000FDE9 (AS_SEQUENCE[65000, 65001])
#   NEXT_HOP: 40030401010101 (1.1.1.1)
# This replaces contains=180A0000, which proved NOTHING: checker.go:616-618 matches
# contains= with a plain strings.Contains over the whole message hex, so the
# peer-down withdraw wire ...0004180A00000000 satisfied it just as well as a real
# announce. This test's green was therefore never evidence that forwarding happened.
# The new bytes cannot be satisfied by any withdraw (a withdraw carries no path
# attributes at all) and additionally prove AC-2/AC-8: the route was forwarded
# through reactorForwardRS with local AS 65000 prepended onto the source's [65001].
# Byte-equality over the FULL UPDATE is the stronger assertion and is deliberately
# NOT used yet: it would also pin the 7 bytes where ze replaces the source's
# ineligible LOCAL_PREF with an ATTR_DISCARD marker, which is the open question
# raised by bgp-rs-fastpath-ebgp-shared.ci -- settle that there first, then tighten
# this to a full hex= match.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:contains=40020A02020000FDE80000FDE940030401010101
EOF_PEER

# Observer: shut down cleanly after forwarding completes.
tmpfs=shutdown-after-up.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
# // test-relax: the observer's synchronous `show bgp summary` eor-sent poll +
# `request shutdown` is replaced by ze_api.run_rs_observer -- event-driven readiness
# (subscribe + wait_for_event on the async channel) plus a fire-and-forget shutdown.
# A more robust rewrite, not a relaxation: same "wait until the RS replayed to every
# peer" semantics, no expect/action/reject assertion removed. The old poll's dispatch
# RPC could stall ~30s under load and strand the shutdown until ze was killed.
from ze_api import API

API().run_rs_observer(expected_peers=2, forward_prefix="10.0.0.0/24")
EOF_RUN

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	external shutdown-after-up {
		run ./shutdown-after-up.run
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
			rs-fast-path enable
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
			rs-fast-path enable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=forward-cached failed
reject=stderr:pattern=release-cached failed
reject=stderr:pattern=BUG: ForwardUpdatesDirect
